    <id>https://doi.org/10.1287/isre.2024.1234</id>
    <title>Generative AI and Price Discrimination in the Housing Market</title>
    <updated>2026-07-23T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Jitsama Tanlamai, Warut Khern-Am-Nuai, Maxime C. Cohen</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Information Systems Research&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Housing price discrimination remains a persistent challenge for fair housing and affordability, with homes in white-dominant neighborhoods priced higher than similar homes in minority-dominant areas. Because pricing decisions increasingly become supported by generative AI (GenAI), this study examines whether GenAI-generated housing prices reinforce or reduce these disparities in the U.S. housing market. Using data on 284,749 U.S. property listings, we compare listed asking prices with prices generated by GenAI. We find that, although GenAI prices are not discrimination free, they reduce the pricing gap between white-dominant and minority-dominant neighborhoods. Further analyses suggest that this reduction is not simply driven by GenAI producing average-like prices or reproducing patterns in numerical housing data. Instead, GenAI pricing appears to reflect broader online content about housing discrimination, which may shape how these models generate housing prices. For real estate professionals, these findings suggest that carefully monitored GenAI tools may help support fairer pricing practices when used to complement, rather than replace, human judgment. For policymakers, the study provides forward-looking evidence to inform how GenAI use in housing-related decision making should be governed to promote fair housing practices.</content>
